  radii are placed at R = R0(model) - (R0 - R)_table = 8.21 - (R0-R), as Bovy & Rix instruct; using their tabulated R (R0 = 8.00) instead shifts every radius by +0.21 kpc.
  THE ITEM ASKED FOR Gaia DR3 K_z(R) OVER R = 6-12 kpc AND NO SUCH TABULATED RUN EXISTS that I could find;
  this is the SEGUE mono-abundance measurement, which is still the standard.  Range covered is 4 kpc of
  radius, and it is INSIDE R0, not outside -- a limitation of the data, not of the prediction.
  [PASS] V5 the interpolated force grid reproduces the exact Hankel evaluation (this is the speed fix that let the script run at all -- it must not cost accuracy)   (max |interp/exact - 1| = 3.83e-04)
  the measured exponential:  K_z,1.1(R0)/2piG = 67.5 Msun/pc^2, scale length 2.69 kpc.
    formal (quoted-error) uncertainties 2.0 and 0.14; BOOTSTRAP over the 43 MAPs gives 1.3 and 0.09, and the exponential's own chi2 = 32/41 d.o.f.
    chi2/dof = 0.79 for a pure exponential means the quoted errors are ADEQUATE.
  [PASS] V6 the refit REPRODUCES Bovy & Rix's own published exponential, which validates the two statistics that item 34's whole verdict rests on   (refit 67.5 Msun/pc^2 and 2.69 kpc against their published K_z,1.1(R0)/2piG = 67 exp(-(R-R0)/2.7 kpc), formal errors 2.5% and 4%)
    significances below use the AUTHORS' formal errors (1.7 Msun/pc^2, 0.11 kpc), which are larger than the bootstrap and include what they could quantify.  McMillan 2017's independently fitted K_z,1.1 corresponds to 73.9 +- 6, and that 10 per cent disagreement BETWEEN measurements is the honest floor on any claim made here.
  
