  THIS IS A PUBLISHED TEST, NOT A NEW ONE, and that has to be said before any verdict: Bovy & Rix devote a
  paragraph of their own Section 7 to exactly this.  They estimate that MOND enhances the vertical force at
  R0 by >~60 per cent, so that for a baryonic column of 51 +- 4 Msun/pc^2 they expect K_z,1.1/2piG >~ 82 +- 6
  against their measured 68 +- 4 -- a '>~2 sigma tension' in their words -- and that the dynamically inferred
  disc scale length should come out ~25 per cent long, ~2.9 kpc, which they call '5 sigma removed' from their
  measured 2.15 +- 0.14 kpc.  What is new here is only that it is redone with THIS kernel and THIS a_0, on
  the full radial run rather than at R0, with the model's own baryonic column (53.4 against their 51 +- 4).
  [FAIL] 34a AGAINST INTEREST -- THE NORMALISATION DOES NOT LAND, AND IT CONFIRMS THE PUBLISHED TENSION RATHER THAN RELIEVING IT.  With a_0 fixed, no halo and nothing fitted, the kernel boost on McMillan's baryonic column gives 88.0 (canonical) and 92.7 (alt) Msun/pc^2 against a measured 67.5 -- 30 and 37 per cent over.  The formal significance is 12 sigma on the authors' own 2.5 per cent error, but THAT NUMBER MUST NOT BE QUOTED: McMillan's independent fit of the same quantity gives 73.9 +- 6, a 10 per cent disagreement between measurements, and against that the honest significance is 2.3 sigma.  Either way it sits just above Bovy & Rix's own MOND estimate of >~82 +- 6, so the exponential kernel does not escape the tension they identified.   (predicted 88.0 (canonical) / 92.7 (alt) vs measured 67.5 +- 1.7 Msun/pc^2 (authors' formal error); against McMillan's independent 73.9 +- 6 it is +2.3 sigma; baryons alone give 60.1, already within 11 per cent)
  [FAIL] 34b AGAINST INTEREST -- THE SHAPE, WHICH IS WHAT THE ITEM ACTUALLY ASKS, DOES NOT LAND EITHER.  The kernel lengthens the predicted scale length (nu rises outward, exactly as the framework says it must), but the baryons were ALREADY too long: predicted 3.98 kpc against a measured 2.69 +- 0.11.  The kernel makes the one discrepancy that existed WORSE, by +0.63 kpc.  The item's own criterion was 10 per cent across the measured range.   (predicted 3.98 kpc (canonical) / 4.04 (alt) vs measured 2.69 +- 0.11; baryons alone 3.36 kpc)
  point by point: median |predicted/measured - 1| = 11.2%, 90th percentile 43.0%, worst 65.2% at R = 7.59 kpc
  [FAIL] 34c the point-by-point agreement misses the item's 10 per cent criterion   (median 11.2%, chi2/N = 3.51)
  predicted K_z scale length as the STELLAR discs are stretched (masses held fixed):
        R_d,thin [kpc]    0.50    1.00    1.50    2.00    2.50    3.00
           h framework    3.38    2.83    2.94    3.40    3.98    4.62
        h baryons only    2.78    2.40    2.52    2.90    3.36    3.84
    the mapping is NOT monotone -- it turns over at R_d = 1.12 kpc, because a very short stellar disc becomes a point mass whose K_z falls faster while the 7 kpc gas disc holds the outer
    column up.  So 'what R_d does the framework need' has no unique answer and the inverse problem is the
    wrong question.  (An earlier version of this block asked it anyway and, because np.interp CLAMPS at the
    ends of a non-monotone grid, it silently returned R_d = 2.88 [2.88, 2.88] kpc -- three identical clamped
    values that I nearly wrote up as a measurement.  The guard that catches it is in invert_h above.)
  the well-posed question instead: FEED THE FRAMEWORK THE DISC BOVY & RIX MEASURE, R_d = 2.15 +- 0.14 kpc.
    framework then predicts a K_z scale length of 3.56 [3.41, 3.73] kpc; baryons alone predict 3.03 kpc; the measurement is 2.69 +- 0.11 kpc
  [FAIL] 34d THE WELL-POSED VERSION, and it is the cleanest form of the liability because it uses only quantities these same 43 stars measure: given Bovy & Rix's own disc, R_d = 2.15 +- 0.14 kpc, the framework predicts a K_z scale length of 3.56 kpc against their measured 2.69 +- 0.11 -- 4.5 sigma long.  Newtonian baryons with the same disc give 3.03 kpc, 1.7 sigma.  The kernel's outward-rising boost inflates the inferred disc, which is exactly the effect Bovy & Rix predicted for MOND at 25 per cent; here it is 18 per cent.   (framework 3.56 [3.41, 3.73] kpc, baryons alone 3.03, measured 2.69 +- 0.11; combined error 0.19; at McMillan's own R_d = 2.50 the framework gives 3.98 kpc)
  LambdaCDM alternative (the SAME baryons + a round NFW halo, r_s = 19 kpc fixed, ONE fitted normalisation): rho_dm(R0) = 0.0096 Msun/pc^3, chi2 = 54
  [FAIL] 34e THE COMPARISON, both ways: on these 43 points the framework's zero-parameter prediction is WORSE than the baryons alone and far worse than baryons plus a one-parameter round halo, which lands on the standard local dark-matter density.  The vertical force at 1.1 kpc is the one place the Milky Way is close to maximal-disc, and that is precisely where a boost is not wanted   (chi2: framework 151 (0 params), baryons alone 135 (0 params), baryons + NFW 54 (1 param, rho_dm(R0) = 0.0096 against the usual 0.008-0.013))
  [FAIL] M34 mutation control, and it FIRES THE WRONG WAY, which is the point: moving a_0 up by a decade (which switches the kernel off) IMPROVES the fit towards the baryons-only value, and switching the kernel off entirely improves it further.  The estimator is working; it is the framework that is being rejected here   (chi2: framework 151, a_0 x10 7350, nu = 1 (baryons alone) 135 -- the mutations are BETTER, not worse)
  [PASS] M34b mutation control: shuffling the measured K_z values between radii destroys the measured scale length, so the scale-length statistic that 34b and 34d turn on carries real radial information   (shuffled scale length -6.79 kpc vs real 2.69 +- 0.11)
  R0 systematic: using the tabulated radii (R0 = 8.00) instead of shifting to the model's 8.21 moves the predicted scale length 3.98 -> 3.97 and the measured 2.69 -> 2.69 kpc; the GAP, which is the result, moves +1.29 -> +1.28 kpc.  The verdict is not an R0 artefact.
  CAVEAT that sets the size of the claim, stated because it is the framework's best defence: K_z^fw = nu(|g_N|) K_z^N is the ALGEBRAIC (QUMOND) estimate with the curl term dropped.  The repository's own
  full AQUAL solve (real_research/reviews/mi_route_a_vertical_radial_ratio_2026.py) finds nu_rad = 1.222 and
  nu_vert = 1.251 at R0 for the alpha=2 kernel, against nu = 1.444 used here -- so the true boost may be ~15 per cent smaller than the algebraic one, which would cut but not
  close the 34a gap (92 -> ~80 against 67.5).  It does NOT touch 34b/34d, because the vertical-to-radial ratio
  that solve reports is 1.024, i.e. essentially constant in R, so it cannot change a SCALE LENGTH.

======================================================================================================================
ITEM 35 -- the Milky Way's claimed Keplerian decline beyond 20 kpc
======================================================================================================================
  two independent measurements of the same curve are run, because the item names both and because a
  verdict that depends on one pipeline is not a verdict.  Systematic fractions are the authors' own:
    Ou+2024      37 points 6.3-27.3 kpc; their stated total systematic 1-5% to R = 22 kpc (4% used), 15% beyond
    Eilers+2019  38 points 5.3-24.8 kpc; their stated total systematic 2-5% throughout (3.5% used)
  EVERY NUMBER BELOW IS QUOTED TWICE, with STAT-only errors and with stat+sys added in quadrature, because
  neither is right on its own: a systematic is COHERENT between radii, so folding it in per point as if it
  were random both flatters the model (chi2 falls) and hides the decline (the slope error inflates).  The
  first version of this section used stat+sys only and reported a comfortable fit; that was my error and it
  is corrected here.  The stat-only column is the SHAPE question the item actually asks.
  the external field the Milky Way actually has: M31's deep-MOND field at 780 kpc is e_N = 0.017 (canonical) / 0.016 (alt) a_0, and the repository's large-scale-structure field for a field galaxy is 0.01-0.03.  The physical prior is e_N <= 0.05, which is exactly
  the number the hunt list wrote down.  e_N is NOT fitted below except to measure what would be required.
  
  Ou+2024   : v_c falls 234.1 -> 173.0 km/s.  Measured outer log-slope over 12-27 kpc = -0.265 +- 0.039 (stat) +- 0.099 (stat+sys)   [flat 0.000, Keplerian -0.500]
     stat      canonical  best fit A = 1.42 (M_b = 8.75e+10 Msun), e_N = 0.000 [0.000, 0.000], chi2/N = 40.3; with e_N = 0 forced, A = 1.42, outer slope -0.154
     stat+sys  canonical  best fit A = 1.42 (M_b = 8.75e+10 Msun), e_N = 0.000 [0.000, 0.008], chi2/N = 0.4; with e_N = 0 forced, A = 1.42, outer slope -0.154
     stat+sys  alt        best fit A = 1.31, e_N = 0.000 [0.000, 0.015], chi2/N = 0.3, e_N=0 slope -0.138
     NOTE the chi2 swings by a factor 90 between the two error models, from 40 to 0.4 per point.  NEITHER is a usable goodness of fit: the random errors alone
     (0.3-2 km/s) are far below both the authors' coherent systematics and the real non-axisymmetric
     structure in the disc, and adding a coherent systematic in quadrature per point then over-corrects.
     Only the SLOPE is quoted as a result below, and the e_N interval only from the stat+sys fit, where
     chi2 per point is of order one and a delta-chi2 = 1 interval means something.
  
  Eilers+2019: v_c falls 231.2 -> 198.4 km/s.  Measured outer log-slope over 12-25 kpc = -0.254 +- 0.056 (stat) +- 0.067 (stat+sys)   [flat 0.000, Keplerian -0.500]
     stat      canonical  best fit A = 1.36 (M_b = 8.39e+10 Msun), e_N = 0.000 [0.000, 0.000], chi2/N = 29.1; with e_N = 0 forced, A = 1.36, outer slope -0.157
     stat+sys  canonical  best fit A = 1.36 (M_b = 8.39e+10 Msun), e_N = 0.000 [0.000, 0.006], chi2/N = 0.6; with e_N = 0 forced, A = 1.36, outer slope -0.157
     stat+sys  alt        best fit A = 1.25, e_N = 0.000 [0.000, 0.008], chi2/N = 0.4, e_N=0 slope -0.140
     NOTE the chi2 swings by a factor 46 between the two error models, from 29 to 0.6 per point.  NEITHER is a usable goodness of fit: the random errors alone
     (0.3-2 km/s) are far below both the authors' coherent systematics and the real non-axisymmetric
     structure in the disc, and adding a coherent systematic in quadrature per point then over-corrects.
     Only the SLOPE is quoted as a result below, and the e_N interval only from the stat+sys fit, where
     chi2 per point is of order one and a delta-chi2 = 1 interval means something.
  
                    measured slope   framework (e_N=0)       gap   sigma
      Ou+2024          -0.265 +-0.039              -0.154    +0.111    +2.8
     Eilers+2019       -0.254 +-0.056              -0.157    +0.098    +1.7
  [PASS] 35a THE ITEM'S PREMISE IS WRONG AND THAT IS THE FIRST RESULT: the outer decline is NOT Keplerian.  The measured log-slopes are -0.265 (Ou) and -0.254 (Eilers) against -0.500 for Kepler, and the two independent pipelines agree with each other to 0.2 sigma.  Any verdict phrased as 'can the framework survive a Keplerian decline' is answering a question these data do not pose.   (measured -0.265 +- 0.039 (Ou) and -0.254 +- 0.056 (Eilers), stat-only; Keplerian is -0.500)
  [FAIL] 35b THE FRAMEWORK DOES MOST BUT NOT ALL OF IT, WITH NO EXTERNAL FIELD AT ALL.  With e_N = 0 and one amplitude, the kernel already produces -0.154 and -0.157 -- 58 and 62 per cent of the observed decline -- because g_bar is still falling at 12-27 kpc and the curve has not reached its asymptote.  What is left over is 2.8 and 1.7 sigma on stat-only errors and 1.1 and 1.5 sigma once the authors' own systematics are folded in.  This is a MILD tension, not a kill, and it is smaller than the item anticipated.   (residual slope tension, stat-only: Ou+2024 +2.8 sigma, Eilers+2019 +1.7 sigma; stat+sys: Ou+2024 +1.1 sigma, Eilers+2019 +1.5 sigma)
  [PASS] 35c THE EXTERNAL FIELD IS NOT NEEDED AND IS NOT DETECTED -- which retires the item's own framing.  Fitting e_N freely returns 0.000 [0, 0.008] (Ou) and 0.000 [0, 0.006] (Eilers): consistent with zero, and the UPPER limits sit at or below the 0.017 that M31 alone supplies.  So the correct statement is the opposite of the one the item expected: these curves put a CEILING on the Milky Way's external field, they do not demand one.   (fitted e_N (stat+sys, canonical) = 0.000 [0, 0.008] and 0.000 [0, 0.006]; alt footing 0.000/0.000; M31 supplies 0.017)
  [FAIL] 35d THE PRICE, AND IT IS THE INHERITED LIABILITY IN ITS SHARPEST FORM: the fit is bought with a baryonic amplitude A = 1.36-1.42 on Ou's own census, i.e. M_b = 8.39-8.75e10 Msun against their 6.16e10 and McMillan's 6.68e10.  The framework needs 25-40 per cent more baryons than the published Milky Way has.  In velocity that is only 6-9 per cent, which is why the curve LOOKS fitted, but it is a real mass discrepancy and it is the same one hunt item 36 recorded   (A = 1.42 (Ou) and 1.36 (Eilers) on M_b = 6.16e+10; required M_b = 8.39e+10-8.75e+10 vs McMillan's 6.68e+10)
  Newtonian baryons alone, with the same one amplitude freedom, give an outer log-slope of -0.520 and need A = 3.3 at 20 kpc -- which is the statement 'dark matter'.
  Ou+2024's own LambdaCDM answer is a fitted CORED EINASTO halo with M_vir = 1.81e11 Msun (their abstract),
  i.e. two shape parameters plus a normalisation, and they say themselves it is in tension with globular-
  cluster, satellite and stream masses.  Bird+2022's NFW fits to halo stars give 0.55-1.00e12.  So the
  decline is uncomfortable for BOTH sides; it is not a clean LambdaCDM win, and it must not be reported as one.
  [PASS] M35 mutation control: shuffling v_c between radii destroys the outer-slope statistic the whole item turns on   (shuffled slope +0.091 vs real -0.265 +- 0.039)
  BOTH WAYS ON THE RESIDUAL 1-3 SIGMA, which is why item 35 is recorded as a MILD TENSION and not as the
  liability the item anticipated: (i) Ou+2024's own systematic
  budget reaches 15 per cent beyond 22 kpc, where the last four points carry the steepest part and have only
  7-22 stars each -- though the authors state explicitly that systematics 'are insufficient to explain the
  decline ... starting at R ~ 15 kpc'; (ii) the two independent pipelines agree on the slope, which removes
  the easy escape that one of them is broken; (iii) a 2025 re-analysis (arXiv:2507.23551) argues the
  recovered potentials are nearly SPHERICAL beyond 20 kpc, contradicting the axisymmetric Jeans equation used
  to derive them, so the steep part may be an artefact.  The liability is recorded anyway: the framework must
  not be allowed to wait for the data to be withdrawn.

