======================================================================================================================
  this is the only genuinely new thing in the three items: 35 and 38 both constrain ONE number, the external
  field e_N, from lever arms a factor of three apart in radius, and 34, 35 and 38a all make a demand on ONE
  number, the baryonic amplitude A.  Asking whether either comes out single-valued is the cross-check.
  
    e_N ALLOWED by the rotation curve at 12-27 kpc   (item 35, Ou)       0.000  [0, 0.008]
    e_N ALLOWED by the rotation curve at 12-25 kpc   (item 35, Eilers)   0.000  [0, 0.006]
    e_N NEEDED  by the halo dispersion at 25-73 kpc  (item 38d, BHB)     0.122
    e_N NEEDED  by the halo dispersion at 25-73 kpc  (item 38c, KG)      unreachable at any e_N
    e_N the Milky Way actually has (M31 + large-scale structure)         0.017 + 0.01-0.03
  [FAIL] CROSS-1 THE EXTERNAL FIELD IS SQUEEZED FROM BOTH SIDES AND THE WINDOW IS EMPTY.  The rotation curve at 12-27 kpc puts a CEILING on it, e_N < 0.008, while the halo dispersion at 25-73 kpc needs a FLOOR of e_N = 0.12 to bend its profile down -- 14 times the ceiling.  One external field cannot do both jobs, so the framework's own escape hatch for the outer Milky Way is closed by the inner Milky Way.  This is new; it is also weak, because the dispersion side of it fails for LambdaCDM too (38c)   (ceiling from item 35 = 0.008 (1 sigma, stat+sys, the looser of the two curves); floor from item 38d = 0.12; M31 supplies 0.017)
  
  amplitude on the published baryonic mass demanded by each lever arm, canonical footing:
     K_z,1.1 at R0            (item 34, |z| = 1.1 kpc, R ~ 8 kpc)   A = 0.77
     v_c at 8.2 kpc           (item 35, in the plane)               A = 1.33   <- the inherited liability
     M(< 73 kpc) halo stars   (item 38a)                            A = 0.82
  [FAIL] CROSS-2 the three lever arms do NOT agree on one baryonic mass, and the pattern is diagnostic: the vertical force at 1.1 kpc wants LESS than the published census (A = 0.77), the halo-star mass at 73 kpc wants about the census (A = 0.82), and the in-plane rotation speed at 8 kpc wants 1.3 times more.  The framework's Milky Way is too flat -- too slow in the plane, too strong above it, and the spread is 1.7x across 8 to 73 kpc.  No single amplitude rescues items 34 and 35 together, so this is a SHAPE problem and adding baryons cannot fix it   (A(K_z,1.1) = 0.77, A(M<73 kpc) = 0.82, A(v_c at R0) = 1.33)
  the v_c normalisation entry is the repository's KNOWN liability (mi_aqual_mcmillan2017_2026.py; hunt item
  36), inherited and not re-discovered here.  What this section adds is that it is one face of a three-sided
  shape problem: the SAME kernel that needs 33 per cent MORE baryons to turn the disc at 8 kpc needs
  23 per cent FEWER to hold the disc down at 1.1 kpc, and those two demands are made of the same stars.
  That is a statement about the VERTICAL-TO-RADIAL ratio of the boost, which the repository's own full AQUAL
  solve puts at 1.024 -- and 1.73 is not 1.02.  A curl-term correction of the size that solve reports cannot
  close it, so the gap lies either in the Milky Way's baryon distribution or in the kernel, not in the algebra.
  
  WHAT SURVIVES, stated as plainly as what does not: item 38a is a genuine zero-parameter success.  The same
  kernel and the same a_0 that fail on the vertical force and on the outer rotation curve turn a 6.7e10 Msun
  baryon census into 4-5e11 Msun of dynamical mass at 52-73 kpc, matching all four independent measurements
  within 1.3 sigma with nothing fitted.  The Milky Way is a hard case for the framework inside 25 kpc
  and an easy one beyond 50, and that radial split is the result of this group.

RESULT: 25 checks, 12 FAIL -> ["34a AGAINST INTEREST -- THE NORMALISATION DOES NOT LAND, AND IT CONFIRMS THE PUBLISHED TENSION RATHER THAN RELIEVING IT.  With a_0 fixed, no halo and nothing fitted, the kernel boost on McMillan's baryonic column gives 88.0 (canonical) and 92.7 (alt) Msun/pc^2 against a measured 67.5 -- 30 and 37 per cent over.  The formal significance is 12 sigma on the authors' own 2.5 per cent error, but THAT NUMBER MUST NOT BE QUOTED: McMillan's independent fit of the same quantity gives 73.9 +- 6, a 10 per cent disagreement between measurements, and against that the honest significance is 2.3 sigma.  Either way it sits just above Bovy & Rix's own MOND estimate of >~82 +- 6, so the exponential kernel does not escape the tension they identified.", "34b AGAINST INTEREST -- THE SHAPE, WHICH IS WHAT THE ITEM ACTUALLY ASKS, DOES NOT LAND EITHER.  The kernel lengthens the predicted scale length (nu rises outward, exactly as the framework says it must), but the baryons were ALREADY too long: predicted 3.98 kpc against a measured 2.69 +- 0.11.  The kernel makes the one discrepancy that existed WORSE, by +0.63 kpc.  The item's own criterion was 10 per cent across the measured range.", "34c the point-by-point agreement misses the item's 10 per cent criterion", "34d THE WELL-POSED VERSION, and it is the cleanest form of the liability because it uses only quantities these same 43 stars measure: given Bovy & Rix's own disc, R_d = 2.15 +- 0.14 kpc, the framework predicts a K_z scale length of 3.56 kpc against their measured 2.69 +- 0.11 -- 4.5 sigma long.  Newtonian baryons with the same disc give 3.03 kpc, 1.7 sigma.  The kernel's outward-rising boost inflates the inferred disc, which is exactly the effect Bovy & Rix predicted for MOND at 25 per cent; here it is 18 per cent.", "34e THE COMPARISON, both ways: on these 43 points the framework's zero-parameter prediction is WORSE than the baryons alone and far worse than baryons plus a one-parameter round halo, which lands on the standard local dark-matter density.  The vertical force at 1.1 kpc is the one place the Milky Way is close to maximal-disc, and that is precisely where a boost is not wanted", 'M34 mutation control, and it FIRES THE WRONG WAY, which is the point: moving a_0 up by a decade (which switches the kernel off) IMPROVES the fit towards the baryons-only value, and switching the kernel off entirely improves it further.  The estimator is working; it is the framework that is being rejected here', "35b THE FRAMEWORK DOES MOST BUT NOT ALL OF IT, WITH NO EXTERNAL FIELD AT ALL.  With e_N = 0 and one amplitude, the kernel already produces -0.154 and -0.157 -- 58 and 62 per cent of the observed decline -- because g_bar is still falling at 12-27 kpc and the curve has not reached its asymptote.  What is left over is 2.8 and 1.7 sigma on stat-only errors and 1.1 and 1.5 sigma once the authors' own systematics are folded in.  This is a MILD tension, not a kill, and it is smaller than the item anticipated.", "35d THE PRICE, AND IT IS THE INHERITED LIABILITY IN ITS SHARPEST FORM: the fit is bought with a baryonic amplitude A = 1.36-1.42 on Ou's own census, i.e. M_b = 8.39-8.75e10 Msun against their 6.16e10 and McMillan's 6.68e10.  The framework needs 25-40 per cent more baryons than the published Milky Way has.  In velocity that is only 6-9 per cent, which is why the curve LOOKS fitted, but it is a real mass discrepancy and it is the same one hunt item 36 recorded", "38c NOT A TEST OF THE FRAMEWORK AT ALL -- MY OWN ESTIMATOR FAILS FIRST, AND SO DOES LambdaCDM.  The measured KG ratio sigma_r(73)/sigma_r(25) = 0.49 is BELOW the pure-Keplerian floor 0.59, which no bounded mass distribution can go under with a single power-law tracer and constant anisotropy.  Bird's own NFW halo, fitted to these very stars, gives 0.79 -- it misses the observed decline by as much as the framework does.  The excess steepness therefore lives in the tracer assumptions (a broken alpha, a rising beta(r), or the exponential fitting function), not in the gravity, and item 38c cannot discriminate.", "38d the ONE thing the dispersion shape does say, and it is against the framework though weakly: the BHB sample's ratio 0.65 IS reachable, and reaching it needs e_N = 0.12 -- above the 0.05 the Milky Way has.  With no external field the framework predicts 0.95, i.e. an almost flat profile, because a logarithmic potential makes sigma_r exactly constant.  The framework's outer halo is too ISOTHERMAL, on both samples, by less than LambdaCDM's own miss.", "CROSS-1 THE EXTERNAL FIELD IS SQUEEZED FROM BOTH SIDES AND THE WINDOW IS EMPTY.  The rotation curve at 12-27 kpc puts a CEILING on it, e_N < 0.008, while the halo dispersion at 25-73 kpc needs a FLOOR of e_N = 0.12 to bend its profile down -- 14 times the ceiling.  One external field cannot do both jobs, so the framework's own escape hatch for the outer Milky Way is closed by the inner Milky Way.  This is new; it is also weak, because the dispersion side of it fails for LambdaCDM too (38c)", "CROSS-2 the three lever arms do NOT agree on one baryonic mass, and the pattern is diagnostic: the vertical force at 1.1 kpc wants LESS than the published census (A = 0.77), the halo-star mass at 73 kpc wants about the census (A = 0.82), and the in-plane rotation speed at 8 kpc wants 1.3 times more.  The framework's Milky Way is too flat -- too slow in the plane, too strong above it, and the spread is 1.7x across 8 to 73 kpc.  No single amplitude rescues items 34 and 35 together, so this is a SHAPE problem and adding baryons cannot fix it"]   rc=1
